qstr: the port may find the firmware's qstrs its own way

qstr_find_strn() runs whenever a str is made (to share a qstr with the
same text) and whenever a name is interned.  It searches the firmware's
pools by a binary search on the strings (and a linear one on the static
pool's hashes): on a board whose firmware is read through a small cache
(the RP2350's XIP), that is some 20 cache misses a str, more than half of
the time of str-heavy code.  Those pools never change, so a port may say
how to find a string in them (MICROPY_PORT_QSTR_FIND_CONST(top pool, str,
len, hash), returning the qstr or MP_QSTRnull): an index by the hash.
Without the macro nothing changes.

--- a/py/qstr.c
+++ b/py/qstr.c
@@ -272,6 +272,14 @@
 
     // search pools for the data
     for (const qstr_pool_t *pool = MP_STATE_VM(last_pool); pool != NULL; pool = pool->prev) {
+        #if defined(MICROPY_PORT_QSTR_FIND_CONST) && MICROPY_QSTR_BYTES_IN_HASH
+        // The firmware's pools (this one and those before it) never change:
+        // the port may find their strings its own way (an index)
+        if (pool == &CONST_POOL) {
+            return MICROPY_PORT_QSTR_FIND_CONST(&CONST_POOL, str, str_len, str_hash);
+        }
+        #endif
+
         size_t low = 0;
         size_t high = pool->len - 1;
 
